Look, a polymorph trap in the mines usually means that sooner or later you are
going to meet a black dragon or an olog-hai just around the next corner.
Splat! - no warning, no chance to do anything about it. That is what the game
is about, and you have to live with it. The resistances are there to avoid
such deaths. Who would need them otherwise?

From my experience nethack does not kill you arbitrairly as often as many 
other similar games. As I pointed out the chances for the kind of arbitrary
death you experienced are probably similar rto the chances of finding a WoW
just lying on the floor...

>>Plus, as others pointed out, you should try to get poison resistanse as soon
>>as possible - there are many poisonous things in nethack, an every of them
>>can be fatal if you are not resistant.
>
>	Operative word being "try."
>
Exactly. That is what nethack is about. There are many RPG's out there that
have a fixed scenario and maps - and there you can ensure that you always can
find everything you need to survive - provided that you correctly follow
the scenario. But I like nethack better because of the randomness it has.
And randomness means that occasionally you encounter poison before you had
a chance to get poison resistance. At least the chances to get poison resistance
before contacting poison are quite good. (and not only barbarians have poison
res at start - healers have it as well).

